  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They went scoreless at home against the Dodgers and never had a chance.",
    "what_this_means": "Yunior Marte gave them a clean five innings and two runs. The kind of start that wins games. And the offense responded with three hits and fourteen strikeouts. Fourteen. That is not a slump. That is a locked door. The Dodgers scored their two runs in the second and then watched the Giants chase. No runners reached scoring position after the third. The bullpen did its job. Hentges, Kuhnel, Smith, and Beck combined for four scoreless innings with no damage. But good pitching only matters when the offense can convert a single opportunity into a run. They didn't get one. This is what happens when the lineup fails to capitalize on a usable start: the team falls back a full game, the division lead grows, and the calendar keeps turning. Three straight losses now. The margin keeps shrinking because the bats keep disappearing.",
    "what_to_watch": "Blake Snell's ERA sits at 1.86 and he is starting tonight for the Dodgers at Oracle, the same ballpark where Matt Wilkinson will need to pitch into quality innings without a single offensive mistake afforded to him.",
